\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Ordered Berezin coefficients}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{V} be an #{N}-dimensional vector space over
#{\mathbb k=\mathbb R} or #{\mathbb C}, with an ordered basis
#{\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_N} of #{V^*}. The
\newvocab{Grassmann algebra} on these generators is the
[exterior algebra](ca-000E) #{\Lambda(V^*)}. Multiplication is
written by juxtaposition, so
##{\xi_i\xi_j=-\xi_j\xi_i,\qquad \xi_i^2=0.}
Its basis consists of #{1} and the increasing words
#{\xi_{i_1}\cdots\xi_{i_r}} with #{i_1<\cdots<i_r}; its
dimension is #{2^N}. A word of length #{r} has degree #{r}.
Elements of even degree commute with every homogeneous element;
two homogeneous elements of degrees #{r,s} commute with sign
#{(-1)^{rs}}.}

\p{The \newvocab{Berezin integral} with reversed top order is
the linear functional
##{I_\xi(f)=\text{coefficient of }\xi_N\cdots\xi_1
       \text{ in }f.}
Thus #{I_\xi(\xi_N\cdots\xi_1)=1} and it vanishes on all
lower degrees. Relative to the increasing top coefficient it
has the sign #{(-1)^{N(N-1)/2}}. For #{N=0} it is the identity
on #{\mathbb k}. This is the purely odd coefficient definition
of \citet{v2, Definition 9.6, p. 128}{etingof2024mathematical},
with the top order stated explicitly.}

\p{For #{n} complex pairs, take #{2n} independent generators
#{\psi_1,\bar\psi_1,\ldots,\psi_n,\bar\psi_n} and use
the paired functional
##{J_n(f)=\text{coefficient of }
  \psi_1\bar\psi_1\cdots\psi_n\bar\psi_n
  \text{ in }f.}
The bars label independent generators, not complex conjugates.
The symbols #{I_\xi} and #{J_n} keep these two top-order
conventions distinct. They are coefficient functionals, not
positive measures on ordinary field configurations.}

\p{For a positive-degree nilpotent element #{q}, define
#{e^q=\sum_{r\ge0}q^r/r!}; only finitely many terms survive.
For a quadratic #{q} in #{N} generators, terms with #{2r>N}
vanish. No analytic convergence is involved. The ordered
monomial basis is supplied formally by
\leanref{Module.Basis.ExteriorAlgebra}; this supports the
algebraic coordinates, not the Berezin or Gaussian identities.}
